<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:sys="clr-namespace:System;assembly=System.Runtime"
                    xmlns:ui="http://schemas.lepo.co/wpfui/2022/xaml">

    <!--
        ZenVizor design tokens. The semantic-name keys (e.g. surface.card,
        accent.default, radius.card, text.subtitle) are the contract between
        Claude Design mockups and Claude Code XAML. Mockup annotations literally
        name the token; this file is where that name resolves to a concrete
        value.

        Sources of truth (see CLAUDE.md "Design system: source of truth"):
          - THIS FILE is canonical for the app.
          - docs/design/colors_and_type.css is canonical for Claude Design mocks.
          - The crosswalk in that CSS header documents value differences and
            is the bridge between the two. The two must not drift; when a
            token value changes here, change it there and vice versa.

        v1 brush strategy: every brush token aliases an existing Wpf.Ui Color
        resource via Color="{DynamicResource <WpfUiColor>}". Result: Wpf.Ui's
        own theme dictionary swap (Light/Dark, OS accent change) re-resolves
        the underlying Color and our aliased brush picks up the new value
        automatically.

        WHY DynamicResource at the Color level (not StaticResource): the
        DynamicResource lookup re-fires on theme changes, so our aliased
        brush picks up the new Color the moment Wpf.Ui swaps its theme
        dictionary. StaticResource would snapshot at load and break theming.
        The aliasing approach is honest about a limit: tokens that the brand
        wants to differ from Wpf.Ui's auto-tinted values (e.g. accent.fill =
        constant brand violet in BOTH themes) carry a CONCRETE Color value
        instead of aliasing — they don't theme-swap, which is the intended
        behavior for accent.fill (AA against white in both themes).

        High contrast: handled by a separate ResourceDictionary
        (HighContrast.xaml) that overrides the semantic tokens onto
        SystemColors brushes when SystemParameters.HighContrast is true. App
        startup is responsible for merging it on/off; see HighContrast.xaml
        header for the wiring contract.

        Dotted x:Key literals (accent.default, surface.card, radius.card) are
        preserved exactly. Do NOT PascalCase them or strip the dots — the
        literal string is the contract that lets a mockup annotation map
        mechanically to a {DynamicResource ...} / {StaticResource ...}
        reference.
    -->

    <!--  Surfaces  ============================================================
          Cards/data surfaces over the Mica backdrop need to be opaque enough
          to guarantee text legibility (WCAG AA). surface.card aliases to
          Wpf.Ui's SolidBackgroundFillColorBase, which is an opaque solid (not
          a translucent fill), so text on top is not subject to Mica showing
          through. surface.background is the page background — Mica's job to
          handle, so it aliases the application background color.

          surface.layer is the OPAQUE flyout/section grouping surface. Per the
          Acrylic decision (see docs/design-system.md), the App-Detail flyout
          is OPAQUE surface.layer, not a frosted panel — WPF has no per-element
          backdrop, and a translucent flyout over Mica would make its text
          contrast wallpaper-dependent. Real Acrylic is reserved for OS-level
          surfaces only (tray menu, context menus). -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.background" Color="{DynamicResource ApplicationBackgroundColor}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.card"       Color="{DynamicResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.card.alt"   Color="{DynamicResource CardBackgroundFillColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.layer"      Color="{DynamicResource LayerFillColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.subtle"     Color="{DynamicResource SubtleFillColorSecondary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.subtle.alt" Color="{DynamicResource SubtleFillColorTertiary}" />
    <!--  surface.tooltip.scrim — base fallback. Brand value is the dark
          text.primary at 84% alpha and lives in
          BrandAccent.{Light,Dark}.xaml (light: #D61A1C26, dark:
          #D6F1F2F8). The fallback here aliases Wpf.Ui's
          SubtleFillColorSecondary so the popover still paints a
          contrasting surface if the brand dict is unmerged — without
          this entry a stray DynamicResource lookup returned null and
          the tooltip background dropped to literal black, swallowing
          the text. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="surface.tooltip.scrim" Color="{DynamicResource SubtleFillColorSecondary}" />

    <!--  Text  ================================================================
          Wpf.Ui's TextFillColor* keys are auto-tinted per theme. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.primary"    Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.secondary"  Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.tertiary"   Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorTertiary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.disabled"   Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorDisabled}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.inverse"    Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorInverse}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="text.on-accent"  Color="{DynamicResource TextOnAccentFillColorPrimary}" />

    <!--  Accent  ==============================================================
          accent.default / .secondary / .tertiary alias Wpf.Ui's SystemAccent*
          Color resources. Those Color values are OVERRIDDEN to brand violet
          by BrandAccent.{Light,Dark}.xaml (merged last in App.xaml), so
          every Wpf.Ui control that paints with accent (NavigationView
          selection, focus rings, Button accent) picks up brand violet
          without per-control work. Theme-swap is automatic via the
          BrandAccent dict swap in App.xaml.cs.

          accent.fill is the brand's answer to the dark-accent contrast issue:
          a CONSTANT brand violet (#6d3fd1 = violet-600) used for accent
          SURFACES that carry on-accent (white) text — filled buttons, pills,
          selection indicators. Constant value in BOTH themes; in dark theme
          this is one stop DARKER than accent.default (which can be lighter
          for text/borders/focus), so white text clears AA 4.5:1 on every
          accent.fill surface regardless of theme. DO NOT use accent.default
          as the background of a filled button — on dark theme it's lighter
          (violet-500 in the brand spec) and fails AA against white. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.default"   Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorPrimary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.secondary" Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorSecondary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.tertiary"  Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorTertiary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.fill"      Color="#6D3FD1" />
    <!-- accent.text — foreground accent text on neutral surface.card.
         Brand spec: violet-700 in light theme / violet-300 in dark, so a 12 px
         body weight clears WCAG AA 4.5:1 against either surface. Used by
         text.eyebrow Style and any other accent-coloured small text.
         Default here aliases SystemAccentColorPrimary so the token resolves
         even without BrandAccent.{Light,Dark}.xaml merged; the brand dict
         overrides with its own SolidColorBrush carrying the violet-700/300
         value. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.text"      Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorPrimary}" />
    <!-- accent.subtle — low-alpha brand violet tint, used as a soft
         selected/hover background for accent surfaces (e.g. the
         NavigationView selected-item background). Theme-swap via
         BrandAccent.xaml; CSS source-of-truth is accent-subtle in
         colors_and_type.css (10% alpha light / 18% alpha dark). Default
         here renders as a translucent accent fallback for non-brand-dict
         sessions. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="accent.subtle"    Color="{DynamicResource SystemAccentColorPrimary}" Opacity="0.10" />

    <!--  Accent fill gradient (filled primary buttons)  ======================
          Metallic violet button face — three-stop pattern: catch-light edge
          band at top (violet-400), main face below (violet-600 = accent.fill),
          darker bottom shade (violet-700). Constant in BOTH themes because
          accent.fill is constant brand violet (violet-600) AA-safe for white
          text on either theme. Paired with .hover and .pressed variants that
          shift one stop lighter / darker respectively, mirroring the brand
          CSS accent-grad / accent-grad-hover variables.

          USAGE: apply via Style="{StaticResource style.button.accent.fill}"
          on a ui:Button. The Style sets Background = metal.accent.fill,
          Foreground = text.on-accent, BorderThickness = 0, and via
          Style.Resources re-publishes ButtonBackgroundPointerOver /
          ButtonBackgroundPressed / ButtonForegroundPointerOver / Pressed /
          ButtonBorderBrushPointerOver so the Wpf.Ui button template picks
          them up at hover/press without any call-site Resources block.

          Two consumers today: ReportsPage Export button (the original
          author of these stops) and AlertsPage filtered-empty Reset CTA.
          Lifted from page-local to canonical per the original ReportsPage
          comment ("promoted to DesignTokens.xaml if it spreads to a second
          consumer"). -->
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="metal.accent.fill" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
        <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#9A72F0" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.1" Color="#6D3FD1" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#561FB0" />
    </LinearGradientBrush>
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="metal.accent.fill.hover" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
        <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#B59AF4" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.1" Color="#8254E6" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#6D3FD1" />
    </LinearGradientBrush>
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="metal.accent.fill.pressed" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
        <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#8254E6" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.1" Color="#561FB0" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#461D7C" />
    </LinearGradientBrush>

    <!--  Status  ==============================================================
          Generic status semantics (success/caution/critical/neutral) plus
          ZenVizor-specific connection-state tokens. The service-status dot in
          MainWindow is hardcoded Brushes.DarkOrange / Brushes.MediumSeaGreen
          today; status.connected / .warming / .disconnected are the tokens
          that should replace those during the polish pass.

          status.caution.text is the AA-safe text colour on a caution tint
          background. The plain status.caution is bright amber (for dots /
          graphics / icon fills); using it as text on the soft caution
          background fails AA at small body sizes in light theme. The brand
          value for caution.text in light is #8A5A00 (~5.2:1 on light tint);
          in dark, the bright amber already passes against the dark tint and
          can be reused, but we encode them separately so the migration to
          theme-aware brushes is mechanical. v1 carries the light value as a
          constant; HighContrast.xaml overrides to SystemColors.ControlTextBrush. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.success"            Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorSuccess}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.success.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorSuccessBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.caution"            Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCaution}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.caution.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCautionBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.caution.text"       Color="#8A5A00" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.critical"           Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCritical}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.critical.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCriticalBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.neutral"            Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorNeutral}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.neutral.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorNeutralBackground}" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.connected"    Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorSuccess}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.warming"      Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCaution}" />
    <!-- status.warming.background is the caution-class banner background, but
         carries its own semantic key so the warming-banner paint can be
         repointed without touching every caution banner in the app. Value
         intentionally identical to status.caution.background. -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.warming.background" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCautionBackground}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="status.disconnected" Color="{DynamicResource SystemFillColorCritical}" />

    <!--  Borders  =============================================================
          Wpf.Ui ships ControlElevationBorderBrush as a LinearGradientBrush
          with NO Color counterpart, so we can't alias it via the Color={...}
          pattern. For card/section strokes we use CardStrokeColorDefault
          (which IS a Color resource). Callers that need the gradient-style
          control border continue to reference Wpf.Ui's
          {DynamicResource ControlElevationBorderBrush} directly. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="border.card"   Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="border.subtle" Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolid}" />

    <!--  Data-viz palette  ====================================================
          SkiaSharp paints used by LiveCharts2 do NOT inherit DynamicResource;
          chart series colors must be applied in code (ChartBuilder) by reading
          these resources via Application.Current.Resources[<key>] and feeding
          them into SKPaints. That wiring is a follow-up; these tokens are the
          contract that wiring code targets.

          Categorical palette (chart.series.1..8, chart.wan, chart.local) is
          the Okabe-Ito colourblind-safe set: deuteranopia + protanopia
          friendly, AA-legible on both Light and Dark Mica. Fixed hex, does
          NOT theme-swap — same in light and dark.

          chart.upSeries and chart.downSeries are the brand's deliberate
          deviation: violet/teal that THEME-SWAP. The brushes here are the
          LIGHT-theme constants used when BrandAccent.{Light,Dark}.xaml has
          not yet merged or has been unmerged for testing; the brand dicts
          override these keys with theme-tuned values
          (violet-600/teal-500 light, violet-400/teal-400 dark) at runtime. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.upSeries"   Color="#6D3FD1" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.downSeries" Color="#20B6C6" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.wan"        Color="#0072B2" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.local"      Color="#009E73" />

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.1" Color="#0072B2" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.2" Color="#E69F00" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.3" Color="#009E73" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.4" Color="#CC79A7" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.5" Color="#56B4E9" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.6" Color="#D55E00" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.7" Color="#F0E442" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.series.8" Color="#999999" />

    <!--  Chart chrome  ========================================================
          LiveCharts2 paints NONE of the axis/gridline/label/tooltip/legend
          chrome from the UI theme — set them explicitly on every chart. These
          tokens are read by ChartBuilder and fed into SKPaints / axis Label
          paints / tooltip styles.

          Mappings from the design-system spec:
            chart.axis         -> border.subtle (faint stroke for axis line)
            chart.gridline     -> border.subtle at lower alpha (set in code:
                                  read the Color and apply alpha ~0x0B)
            chart.axis.label   -> text.tertiary (de-emphasized label text)
            chart.tooltip.bg   -> surface.card (OPAQUE — never translucent)
            chart.tooltip.text -> text.primary
            chart.legend.text  -> text.secondary

          All theme-swapping (they alias theme-swapping Wpf.Ui Colors). -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.axis"         Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolid}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.gridline"     Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefault}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.axis.label"   Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorTertiary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.tooltip.bg"   Color="{DynamicResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.tooltip.text" Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimary}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="chart.legend.text"  Color="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondary}" />

    <!--  Spacing scale  =======================================================
          4-based scale, named by pixel value. App set {4,8,12,16,24,32,48} is
          a clean subset of the brand scale; brand adds {20,40,64}. Use
          space.12 (not space.10) for medium spacing — keeps rhythm.

          USAGE NOTE: do NOT write Margin="{StaticResource space.16}" or
          Padding="{StaticResource space.16}". ThicknessConverter only
          converts from String — the sys:Double resource returns a boxed
          Double which WPF can't coerce to Thickness, and the page fails
          to load at runtime. Write the literal value instead
          (Margin="16", Padding="16"); XAML's string TypeConverter handles
          it. The token values here are the documentation of the canonical
          values — match them by hand at call-sites. -->

    <sys:Double x:Key="space.4">4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.8">8</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.12">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.16">16</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.20">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.24">24</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.32">32</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.40">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.48">48</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="space.64">64</sys:Double>

    <!--  Corner radius  =======================================================
          BRAND values (reconciled from the placeholder Wpf.Ui ladder). The
          crosswalk in docs/design/colors_and_type.css records the migration
          (radius.sm 4->6, radius.md 6->10, radius.lg 8->14, radius.xs new at
          4 for backwards compat with the old radius.sm value).

          SPLIT into scale + semantic roles. Reason: Wpf.Ui's controls share
          a single CornerRadius resource for both controls and cards; pushing
          radius.md to 10 (cards) would drag button/input radius along if
          anything pointed them at radius.md. The semantic role tokens
          (radius.control / radius.card / radius.overlay) name the INTENT, so
          future scale tuning never affects the wrong surface — and the
          eventual override of Wpf.Ui's ControlCornerRadius can point at
          radius.control without ambiguity.

          Usage:
            - Buttons, inputs, chips, inline banners: radius.control (6)
            - Cards, list rows:                       radius.card (10)
            - Flyouts, dialogs, overlays:             radius.overlay (14)
            - Bare scale values exist for one-off needs but prefer the role
              tokens at call-sites.

          Migration note for Wpf.Ui controls: brand wants controls at 6px
          (currently render at Wpf.Ui's 4px). The override is in App.xaml
          merged dictionaries AFTER Wpf.Ui's theme dict:
              <CornerRadius x:Key="ControlCornerRadius">6</CornerRadius>
          (or point ControlCornerRadius at radius.control via an alias). This
          is deferred until the polish-pass call-site sweep so the visual jump
          is auditable in one diff. -->

    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.xs">4</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.sm">6</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.md">10</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.lg">14</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.xl">20</CornerRadius>
    <!-- radius.pill is a sentinel — XAML has no native "fully rounded" value.
         At call-sites that need a pill, set CornerRadius to half the control
         height directly (e.g. a 24-tall chip uses CornerRadius="12"). 9999 is
         the conventional pill value and renders rounded for any control
         height up to that size. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.pill">9999</CornerRadius>

    <!-- Semantic role tokens. These are the keys to USE at call-sites. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.control">6</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.card">10</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="radius.overlay">14</CornerRadius>

    <!--  Typography scale  ====================================================
          BRAND sizes (reconciled from the smaller Fluent ladder shipping
          today). The crosswalk in colors_and_type.css records each delta.

          Only font.size.display renders in font.brand (Nuqun); everything
          from title.large down renders in font.display (Urbanist). -->

    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.caption">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.body">14</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.body.large">18</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.subtitle">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.metric">24</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.title">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.title.large">40</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="font.size.display">68</sys:Double>

    <FontWeight x:Key="font.weight.regular">Normal</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="font.weight.semibold">SemiBold</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="font.weight.bold">Bold</FontWeight>

    <!--  Type ramp Styles  ====================================================
          Keyed TextBlock Styles with ABSOLUTE LineHeight values computed from
          the brand line-height ratios (display 1.12, title-large 1.20, title
          1.28, subtitle 1.40, body-* 1.50, caption 1.40).

          WHY absolute LineHeight not multipliers: WPF's TextBlock.LineHeight
          takes a Double in DIPs, not a multiplier. Setting it explicitly
          gives consistent vertical rhythm across pages even when the font
          falls back. LineStackingStrategy=BlockLineHeight makes LineHeight
          authoritative — without it, ascenders/descenders that overshoot the
          declared line height push lines apart and the rhythm drifts.

          Apply via Style="{StaticResource text.subtitle}" etc. The styles
          target plain WPF TextBlock; the implicit base TextBlock style below
          still sets Foreground app-wide, and these keyed styles inherit from
          nothing (so they don't have to opt in). -->

    <Style x:Key="text.display" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.brand}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="76" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.title.large" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.title.large}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="48" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.title" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.title}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="36" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.subtitle" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.subtitle}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="28" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <!-- Metric: compact display value for headline rate / count cards.
         24 px SemiBold sits between text.subtitle (20) and text.title (28),
         giving status cards a prominent value without the 40 px overflow
         of text.title.large. Pair with FontFamily=font.mono override at the
         call site for numeric values where digit alignment matters. -->
    <Style x:Key="text.metric" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.metric}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="32" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.body.large" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body.large}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="27" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.body.strong" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="21" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.body" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="21" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <Style x:Key="text.caption" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.caption}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="17" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}" />
    </Style>
    <!-- Eyebrow: uppercase, tracked, semibold caption — used for tiny
         section labels above a title. Foreground = accent.text token —
         brand violet-700 (light) / violet-300 (dark), tuned for AA contrast
         on surface.card at body-caption weight. Wired via BrandAccent.xaml. -->
    <Style x:Key="text.eyebrow" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.display}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.caption}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.semibold}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="17" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource accent.text}" />
        <!-- WPF has no text-transform: uppercase the source string at the
             call-site (e.g. Text="STATUS"). WPF also has no letter-spacing
             on TextBlock/TextElement — the brand's tracking-wide (0.04em)
             on eyebrows can't be applied here without per-character Runs.
             Accepted limitation; visually OK because eyebrows are small,
             short, and always uppercase. -->
    </Style>
    <!-- Mono / data: Overpass Mono with tabular numerics. Used for rates,
         byte counts, IPs, ports, PIDs — anywhere column alignment of
         digits matters. font.mono is a variable font (weight axis 100-900)
         but this Style pins Regular for the canonical look. -->
    <Style x:Key="text.mono" TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource font.mono}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />
        <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="{StaticResource font.weight.regular}" />
        <Setter Property="LineHeight" Value="21" />
        <Setter Property="LineStackingStrategy" Value="BlockLineHeight" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>

    <!--  Motion  ===============================================================
          Animation primitives — durations and easing functions. Theme-
          invariant (motion is not a visual surface), so StaticResource at
          call-sites is fine; HighContrast.xaml does NOT override these
          (consumers gate animation on SystemParameters.ClientAreaAnimation
          at the code-behind layer, which is the canonical Windows path).

          motion.duration.arrival (600ms) — the one-shot pulse-ring duration
          for nav-rail badge arrival cues. Long enough to read across the
          full nav rail at the badge's small size; dur.slow (320ms) is too
          quick to track at that scale. Source: alerts mockup page 14.

          motion.duration.fast (200ms) — the item-level ring-glow cue when
          a new alert inserts at the top of the Alerts feed. Light-and-fast
          per the primer; one-shot, never looped.

          motion.ease.glide (CubicEase EaseOut) — the brand-aerodynamic
          ease curve. Matches the CSS ease-glide variable cubic-bezier(0.22,
          0.61, 0.36, 1) closely enough at audit-level; CubicEase EaseOut
          is the closest stock WPF easing to that curve.

          USAGE: durations are sys:TimeSpan (Storyboard.Duration takes a
          Duration struct, but C# implicit conversion from TimeSpan does
          the right thing when assigning in code-behind:
            var dur = (TimeSpan)Application.Current.Resources["motion.duration.arrival"];
            anim.Duration = dur;  // implicit TimeSpan -> Duration). -->

    <sys:TimeSpan x:Key="motion.duration.arrival">0:0:0.6</sys:TimeSpan>
    <sys:TimeSpan x:Key="motion.duration.fast">0:0:0.2</sys:TimeSpan>
    <CubicEase x:Key="motion.ease.glide" EasingMode="EaseOut" />

    <!--  style.button.accent.fill  ============================================
          Canonical Style for filled primary (accent) buttons. Background
          paints metal.accent.fill (the violet three-stop gradient);
          hover/pressed states paint the matching .hover / .pressed
          gradients via Style.Resources override of Wpf.Ui's WinUI 3 brush
          keys (ButtonBackgroundPointerOver / Pressed). Foreground locks
          to text.on-accent (white) across base / hover / pressed so the
          label stays readable on every violet face.

          BasedOn the implicit ui:Button style so Wpf.Ui's button chrome
          (corner radius, padding defaults, focus visuals) is inherited;
          only the brush-key overrides + background/foreground/border
          setters change.

          Consumers: ReportsPage ExportButton; AlertsPage filtered-empty
          Reset CTA (Phase 6). Promoted from ReportsPage local Resources
          on 2026-06-13 per its original author's "second-consumer
          promotion" note. -->
    <Style x:Key="style.button.accent.fill" TargetType="ui:Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type ui:Button}}">
        <Style.Resources>
            <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPointerOver" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
                <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#B59AF4" />
                <GradientStop Offset="0.1" Color="#8254E6" />
                <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#6D3FD1" />
            </LinearGradientBrush>
            <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPressed" StartPoint="0,0" EndPoint="0,1">
                <GradientStop Offset="0"   Color="#8254E6" />
                <GradientStop Offset="0.1" Color="#561FB0" />
                <GradientStop Offset="1"   Color="#461D7C" />
            </LinearGradientBrush>
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonForegroundPointerOver" Color="White" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonForegroundPressed" Color="White" />
            <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBorderBrushPointerOver" Color="Transparent" />
        </Style.Resources>
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource metal.accent.fill}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource text.on-accent}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!--  brand.menu.item  ======================================================
          Canonical MenuItem Style for the brand ContextMenu treatment.
          BasedOn the implicit ui:MenuItem style so Wpf.Ui's hover / selection
          / focus chrome and IsCheckable checkmark visual are inherited; the
          Setters add the mockup-aligned Padding + inter-item Margin that the
          default Wpf.Ui ContextMenu ships too tight.

          Consumers: ReportsPage Export + Anchor ContextMenus; AlertsPage
          Type filter ContextMenu. Promoted from ReportsPage local Resources
          when AlertsPage became the second consumer — same "promote on
          second consumer" pattern metal.accent.fill followed.  -->
    <Style x:Key="brand.menu.item"
           TargetType="MenuItem"
           BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type MenuItem}}">
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="18,12" />
        <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,4" />
    </Style>

    <!--  Density  =============================================================
          Default Fluent grid spacing is too airy for ZenVizor's data-dense
          surfaces (per-app list, sessions, connections). The compact variant
          is the one to apply on those DataGrids. Apply via:

              <DataGrid Style="{StaticResource style.datagrid.compact}" ... />

          density.row.default / .compact are exposed as raw Doubles for cases
          where a custom row template needs to read the row height directly.

          Row height tuning (Per-App polish, 2026-06-06): the original 22px
          compact target was too tight at 14px body — text crowded the cell
          edges and headers needed visible breathing room. Bumped to 32px.
          Default stays at 28 — not currently used at any call-site, kept
          as a reference value.

          Cell Padding is HORIZONTAL-ONLY (12,0) — never add vertical
          padding here. Rows are fixed-height, so vertical placement comes
          entirely from the ContentPresenter's VerticalAlignment=Center;
          vertical padding buys nothing visually but SHRINKS the content
          slot (rowHeight − 2·padV). The moment that slot drops below the
          cell TextBlock's desired height, WPF applies its layout clip
          (ArrangeCore sets NeedsClipBounds whenever desiredSize > arrange
          slot — ClipToBounds="False" does NOT opt out of this) and glyph
          ink is sheared off at the slot edge. The recurring Per-App
          descender clipping (bottom of g/p/q/y missing; fixed 2026-06-11)
          was exactly this: Padding=12,8 left a 16px slot for a 27px-tall
          TextBlock, and the clip plane landed mid-descender. Measured
          Urbanist@14px facts for future tuning: natural line box 16.8px,
          baseline 13.3px from line-box top, descender ink ends at exactly
          16.8px — flush with the line-box bottom, zero slack — so ANY
          clip plane at or above the line-box bottom eats descenders.

          IMPORTANT: the RowStyle + CellStyle below own the hover /
          selection / pill chrome explicitly. Earlier attempts to BasedOn
          Wpf.Ui's named keys (DefaultDataGridCellStyle and
          DefaultUiDataGridCellStyle) both failed empirically — the
          IsSelected and IsMouseOver triggers never fired. Hypothesis: the
          BasedOn chain didn't resolve to a Style that carries the brand
          chrome at our resource scope; Wpf.Ui's implicit cell/row styles
          may differ from their keyed siblings. Rather than chase the right
          key, we ship our own minimal templates so the brand visuals are
          guaranteed regardless of upstream Wpf.Ui changes. Trade-off: we
          opt out of any future Wpf.Ui DataGrid styling refinements; we
          accept that — a stable visual on a stable token surface is more
          valuable than implicit inheritance from a moving target. -->

    <sys:Double x:Key="density.row.default">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="density.row.compact">32</sys:Double>

    <Style x:Key="style.datagrid.compact" TargetType="DataGrid">
        <Setter Property="RowHeight" Value="{StaticResource density.row.compact}" />
        <Setter Property="MinRowHeight" Value="{StaticResource density.row.compact}" />
        <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{StaticResource font.size.body}" />

        <!--  CellStyle — minimal: padding + simple Border-wrapped
              ContentPresenter. Hover / selection chrome lives on RowStyle
              below (so the whole row paints, not just individual cells). -->
        <Setter Property="CellStyle">
            <Setter.Value>
                <Style TargetType="DataGridCell">
                    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="12,0" />
                    <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                    <Setter Property="FocusVisualStyle" Value="{x:Null}" />
                    <Setter Property="Template">
                        <Setter.Value>
                            <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridCell">
                                <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                        Padding="{TemplateBinding Padding}"
                                        SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                                    <ContentPresenter VerticalAlignment="Center"
                                                      SnapsToDevicePixels="{TemplateBinding SnapsToDevicePixels}" />
                                </Border>
                            </ControlTemplate>
                        </Setter.Value>
                    </Setter>
                </Style>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>

        <!--  RowStyle — carries all the brand selection / hover chrome:
              * StateOverlay sits BEHIND the cells presenter and tints on
                IsMouseOver (surface.subtle) / IsSelected (accent.subtle).
                Cells are transparent so the overlay shows through.
              * SelectionPill is a 3px-wide accent.default left edge that
                flips Visible on IsSelected. Visually a row-level "selected"
                marker, mirroring the NavigationView selection idiom.
              * IsHitTestVisible=False on both overlays so mouse events
                still hit the cells beneath (selection + drill semantics
                unchanged).
              Background TemplateBinding is preserved so DataGrid's
              RowBackground / AlternatingRowBackground continue to paint
              the alt-row tint behind the StateOverlay. -->
        <Setter Property="RowStyle">
            <Setter.Value>
                <Style TargetType="DataGridRow">
                    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="Transparent" />
                    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="0" />
                    <!--  Hand cursor telegraphs the single-click drill affordance
                          that style.datagrid.compact grids carry. Currently every
                          consumer of this style is a drill surface (Per-App
                          today); future non-drill compact grids would need to
                          opt out by overriding RowStyle.Cursor inline. See
                          memory: feedback_drill_grid_pattern.md. -->
                    <Setter Property="Cursor" Value="Hand" />
                    <Setter Property="Template">
                        <Setter.Value>
                            <ControlTemplate TargetType="DataGridRow">
                                <Border Background="{TemplateBinding Background}"
                                        BorderBrush="{TemplateBinding BorderBrush}"
                                        BorderThickness="{TemplateBinding BorderThickness}"
                                        SnapsToDevicePixels="True">
                                    <Grid>
                                        <Border x:Name="StateOverlay"
                                                Background="Transparent"
                                                IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                                        <DataGridCellsPresenter ItemsPanel="{TemplateBinding ItemsPanel}"
                                                                SnapsToDevicePixels="{TemplateBinding SnapsToDevicePixels}" />
                                        <Border x:Name="SelectionPill"
                                                Width="3"
                                                HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                                                Background="{DynamicResource accent.default}"
                                                Visibility="Collapsed"
                                                IsHitTestVisible="False" />
                                    </Grid>
                                </Border>
                                <ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                                    <Trigger Property="IsMouseOver" Value="True">
                                        <Setter TargetName="StateOverlay" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource surface.subtle}" />
                                    </Trigger>
                                    <Trigger Property="IsSelected" Value="True">
                                        <Setter TargetName="StateOverlay" Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource accent.subtle}" />
                                        <Setter TargetName="SelectionPill" Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                                    </Trigger>
                                </ControlTemplate.Triggers>
                            </ControlTemplate>
                        </Setter.Value>
                    </Setter>
                </Style>
            </Setter.Value>
        </Setter>
    </Style>

    <!--  Metallic-card + elevation base fallbacks  ===============================
          metal.card / metal.control / edge.light / shadow.sm / shadow.card
          carry their REAL brand values in BrandAccent.{Light,Dark}.xaml
          (metal.card and metal.control are LinearGradientBrushes; the
          shadows are tuned DropShadowEffects). Views DynamicResource them
          on every data-bearing card — without a base entry, an unmerged
          brand dict (HC mode, future test harnesses, a debugging session
          with the brand dict commented out) resolved to null and the
          card silently lost its surface entirely (transparent over Mica).
          The fallbacks here paint a clean opaque card so the surface
          degrades gracefully:
            - metal.card → surface.card (flat opaque card brush)
            - metal.control → surface.card (flat control surface)
            - edge.light → border.subtle (lighter divider stroke;
              invisible on white-on-white in light theme, faint top
              catch-light in dark — same visual character the brand dict
              composites into metal.card itself)
            - shadow.sm / shadow.card → null effect (no shadow rather
              than a guessed wrong one; views still render correctly).
          HighContrast.xaml overrides every key on this list to its
          SystemColors equivalent. -->

    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="metal.card"    Color="{DynamicResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="metal.control" Color="{DynamicResource SolidBackgroundFillColorBase}" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="edge.light"    Color="{DynamicResource CardStrokeColorDefaultSolid}" />
    <!-- Inert DropShadowEffect fallbacks. Opacity=0 means cards render
         without elevation if the brand dict is unmerged; better than a
         non-fallback (would crash the view trying to resolve the missing
         resource on Effect={DynamicResource ...}). -->
    <DropShadowEffect x:Key="shadow.sm"   Opacity="0" />
    <DropShadowEffect x:Key="shadow.card" Opacity="0" />

    <!--  Implicit base-TextBlock style  ===========================================
          WPF's plain <TextBlock>.Foreground defaults to literal Brushes.Black
          (NOT a DynamicResource), so any TextBlock without an explicit
          Foreground renders black regardless of theme. Wpf.Ui's <ui:TextBlock>
          themes itself via its own implicit style, but plain WPF TextBlocks
          do not — that's what made the Dashboard column headers ("App",
          "Publisher", ...) render dark-on-dark in dark mode.

          An implicit style targeting WPF's TextBlock fixes this app-wide
          without touching every call-site. Implicit styles only apply to the
          EXACT type, so this does NOT shadow <ui:TextBlock>'s style. Any
          TextBlock that sets Foreground locally also wins via WPF property
          precedence (local value > setter). Keyed text.* styles above use
          BasedOn=null implicitly (no explicit BasedOn) and set Foreground
          themselves, so they win when applied. -->

    <Style TargetType="TextBlock">
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextFillColorPrimaryBrush}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
